A vulnerability exists where a zero-permission background app can
bypass the TURN_SCREEN_ON permission to repeatedly wake the
physical screen. This is achieved by creating a
TYPE_PRIVATE_PRESENTATION window with FLAG_TURN_SCREEN_ON on a
private virtual display.

Previously, in WindowState.prepareWindowToDisplayDuringRelayout(),
the check for whether a window could turn the screen on would
short-circuit to true if `mActivityRecord` was null. This allowed
non-activity windows on zero-permission virtual displays to bypass
the one-shot restriction (`currentLaunchCanTurnScreenOn`). Because
zero-permission virtual displays fall back to the DEFAULT_DISPLAY_GROUP,
this resulted in waking the physical device screen.

This commit resolves the issue by updating the logic so that the
screen can only be turned on trusted displays.

